---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
---

<Layout title="privacy · kindling" description="kindling privacy policy">
  <div class="mx-auto flex min-h-dvh max-w-[640px] flex-col px-6 py-16">
    <a href="/" class="mb-12">
      <Logo />
    </a>
    <h1 class="text-[32px] font-medium tracking-[-0.8px]">privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[16px] tracking-[-0.4px] text-muted">last updated Aug 20, 2026</p>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-[16px] leading-relaxed tracking-[-0.4px] text-black/80">
      <p>
        kindling is made by jan rebolledo. this page is the whole policy: what the app uses,
        why, and who else sees it. we don’t sell your data, we don’t run ads, and we don’t want
        your screenshots sitting on our servers.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">screenshots</h2>
      <p>
        when you scan screenshots, kindling reads the text on your iphone. the photos stay on
        your phone. we never upload them.
      </p>
      <p>
        by default, that text is sent to google’s gemini so it can find places and events, and
        pull out details like tips. you can instead choose “on this iphone” in settings to use
        apple’s foundation model without sending the screenshot text to kindling or google. in
        that mode, kindling receives only the extracted place or event details needed to look it
        up and save it. we don’t keep the full screenshot text. google may use text processed in
        cloud mode to improve gemini and their other products, per their
        <a
          class="underline decoration-solid underline-offset-2"
          href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms">gemini terms</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">photo identifiers</h2>
      <p>
        to show your original screenshot next to a saved place, we store an id for that photo
        on your account — the id your phone uses, not the image. that’s the only reason we keep
        it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">account</h2>
      <p>
        you sign in with apple. we store your name, the email apple shares (if any), and a
        username so you can use the app, plus a couple of preferences like how you like to get
        directions. account data lives in supabase.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">saved places</h2>
      <p>
        places you save — a title, description, category, event details, a photo, and a Google
        Places identifier — live with your account. kindling retrieves current place details such
        as the address, hours, venue name, photos, and route estimates from Google when needed.
        that’s your list, not a copy of your screenshots.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">location</h2>
      <p>
        if you allow it, kindling uses your location on your phone to request a route estimate
        from Google Routes through kindling’s API. we don’t store your live location.
      </p>
      <p>
        you can turn off photo or location access anytime in ios settings → kindling.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">sharing</h2>
      <p>
        if you share an idea from the app, we publish its name, description, optional media, and
        highlights at a public link. your screenshot is not included.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">who else sees data</h2>
      <p>
        apple (sign in and on-device foundation models), supabase (account and saved places),
        google (Places and Routes, plus Gemini for screenshot text in cloud mode), and cloudflare
        (hosts the app). supabase, google, gemini, and cloudflare are required under their terms
        to protect this data. we keep logs for up to 7 days to debug the app.
        they don’t contain screenshot images or the full screenshot text — just technical info
        like error messages, and sometimes venue names. we don’t use analytics or ad trackers.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">deletion</h2>
      <p>
        you can delete your account from the app at any time, or
        <a
          class="underline decoration-solid underline-offset-2"
          href="mailto:contact@janrebolledo.com">email us</a
        >
        and we’ll do it. that wipes everything tied to you.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">kids</h2>
      <p>kindling isn’t for anyone under 18, and we don’t knowingly collect their data.</p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">changes</h2>
      <p>if this policy changes, we’ll update this page with a new date.</p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-[20px] font-medium tracking-[-0.5px] text-black">contact</h2>
      <p>
        questions:
        <a
          class="underline decoration-solid underline-offset-2"
          href="mailto:contact@janrebolledo.com">contact@janrebolledo.com</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-auto pt-16">
      <Footer />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
